public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
    {
        AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

        int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int Old_x = x;
        int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
        bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

        int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
        bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
        PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

        if (New_Ret >= 0)
        {
            // @@@ Assert the size of old and new list is same
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
        }
    }
}
</code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
</code>

As in the PUT_LastIndexOfContract method, the return value is negative if the specified object is not found in the ArrayList, or if the ArrayList contains multiple instances of the object. Because the method is supposed to return the last occurrence of an element, the return value must be the element index if it's found, or -1 otherwise. Therefore the result of the New_Ret has to be greater or equal to 0. 

Additionally, we know that the Count property of ArrayList will remain the same if the item that was added does not affect the positioning of other items, in such case the size of the ArrayList should remain the same before and after the operation. 

In the PUT_LastIndexOfContract method, the LastIndexOf method was called with a value, it may or may not be contained in the ArrayList. This is what the assertion checks: if the method returns a -1 or a value greater or equal to 0, the ArrayList's count remains same before and after the LastIndexOf method call and the item that was searched is contained in the ArrayList.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >=0 ? Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount : true);
Debug.Assert(Old_x == New_x);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX);
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);
</code> 

In the given code, we first verify if New_Ret is not negative (i.e., the method lastIndexOf was called successfully), and then check that the sizes of the old and new list are the same. After that, we verify that all other values remain the same as in the old state (Old_x, Old_arrListIndexOfX, and Old_arrListContainsX). Thus, the assert statement is checking that the size of the list remains the same or that the method was not successful, while maintaining the original state of the variables, and therefore ensuring the correctness of the code.

The output is dependent on your programming environment, some are using Debug statements in C#. Debugging can be set to break the program, making it easier to trace errors or find where code blocks are being executed. These Debug statements are removed when your build's configuration is set to Release, so it's crucial to include this check when you are ready to ship your application.--------------------

    Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 ? Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount : new_arrList_Count > Old_arrListCount);

